 "response": "Here are several ways to write the `is_palindrome(s)` function in Python, depending on your preferred style (concise, readable, or performance-focused).\n\n## Option 1: The Standard, Pythonic Way (Recommended)\n\nThis method is the most common and easiest to read. It first cleans the string (removes spaces and makes it lowercase) and then compares it to its reversed version.\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string is a palindrome, ignoring case and non-alphanumeric characters.\n    \n    Args:\n        s: The input string.\n        \n    Returns:\n        True if the string is a palindrome, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    # 1. Normalize the string: keep only alphanumeric characters and convert to lowercase\n    cleaned_s = \"\".join(char for char in s if char.isalnum()).lower()\n    \n    # 2. Compare the cleaned string to its reverse\n    return cleaned_s == cleaned_s[::-1]\n\n# --- Examples ---\nprint(f\"'Racecar' is a palindrome: {is_palindrome('Racecar')}\")\nprint(f\"'A man, a plan, a canal: Panama' is a palindrome: {is_palindrome('A man, a plan, a canal: Panama')}\")\nprint(f\"'hello world' is a palindrome: {is_palindrome('hello world')}\")\nprint(f\"'Madam' is a palindrome: {is_palindrome('Madam')}\")\nprint(f\"'12321' is a palindrome: {is_palindrome('12321')}\")\n```\n\n---\n\n## Option 2: Two-Pointer Approach (Most Efficient)\n\nFor maximum efficiency (especially with very long strings, as it avoids creating a fully cleaned, reversed string), you can use a \"two-pointer\" approach. This involves comparing characters from the start and the end, moving inwards, until the pointers meet.\n\n```python\ndef is_palindrome_two_pointers(s: str) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if a string is a palindrome using the two-pointer technique.\n    This is often the most memory-efficient method.\n    \"\"\"\n    left = 0\n    right = len(s) - 1\n\n    while left < right:\n        # Move the pointers inward past any non-alphanumeric characters\n        if not s[left].isalnum():\n            left += 1\n            continue\n        if not s[right].isalnum():\n            right -= 1\n            continue\n            \n        # Compare the normalized characters\n        if s[left].lower() != s[right].lower():\n            return False\n        \n        # If they match, move both pointers inward\n        left += 1\n        right -= 1\n        \n    return True\n```\n\n---\n\n## Comparison Summary\n\n| Method | Pros | Cons | Complexity (Time) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Option 1 (Slicing)** | Extremely concise and Pythonic. Easy to understand. | Requires creating two new strings (cleaned and reversed). | $O(N)$ |\n| **Option 2 (Two Pointers)** | Very memory efficient. Only uses a few variables. | More code lines; the logic is slightly harder to grasp initially. | $O(N)$ |\n\n**Recommendation:** For most general applications, **Option 1** is preferred for its readability and brevity. If you are dealing with constraints where memory usage is critical, use **Option 2**.",
